{
 "duration": 107.31,
 "segments": [
  {
   "i": 0,
   "start": 0.0,
   "end": 1.15,
   "text": "Two price tags."
  },
  {
   "i": 1,
   "start": 1.6,
   "end": 3.37,
   "text": "One says nine ninety-nine."
  },
  {
   "i": 2,
   "start": 3.79,
   "end": 5.38,
   "text": "The other says ten dollars."
  },
  {
   "i": 3,
   "start": 5.87,
   "end": 7.49,
   "text": "The difference is one cent."
  },
  {
   "i": 4,
   "start": 7.99,
   "end": 10.06,
   "text": "So why do they feel so far apart?"
  },
  {
   "i": 5,
   "start": 10.98,
   "end": 14.19,
   "text": "Walk through any store and the nine is everywhere."
  },
  {
   "i": 6,
   "start": 14.72,
   "end": 18.0,
   "text": "In the data behind one economics study of a US retailer,"
  },
  {
   "i": 7,
   "start": 18.29,
   "end": 22.59,
   "text": "roughly a quarter to a third of all prices ended in ninety-nine cents."
  },
  {
   "i": 8,
   "start": 23.09,
   "end": 23.96,
   "text": "That isn't habit."
  },
  {
   "i": 9,
   "start": 24.37,
   "end": 26.74,
   "text": "It's a bet on how your mind reads a number."
  },
  {
   "i": 10,
   "start": 27.38,
   "end": 28.26,
   "text": "Here's the mechanism."
  },
  {
   "i": 11,
   "start": 28.75,
   "end": 30.52,
   "text": "We read prices left to right,"
  },
  {
   "i": 12,
   "start": 30.78,
   "end": 32.73,
   "text": "and the first digit does most of the work."
  },
  {
   "i": 13,
   "start": 33.22,
   "end": 35.61,
   "text": "Researchers call it the left-digit effect."
  },
  {
   "i": 14,
   "start": 36.24,
   "end": 44.16,
   "text": "In 2005, Manoj Thomas and Vicki Morwitz found that two ninety-nine registers as closer to two dollars than to three."
  },
  {
   "i": 15,
   "start": 44.63,
   "end": 47.5,
   "text": "And it only kicks in when that first digit changes."
  },
  {
   "i": 16,
   "start": 48.12,
   "end": 50.52,
   "text": "It shows up where real money is on the line."
  },
  {
   "i": 17,
   "start": 51.04,
   "end": 56.56,
   "text": "In more than twenty-two million used-car sales, prices dropped at every ten-thousand-mile mark."
  },
  {
   "i": 18,
   "start": 57.15,
   "end": 62.97,
   "text": "A car just under eighty thousand miles sold for about two hundred ten dollars more than one just over."
  },
  {
   "i": 19,
   "start": 63.45,
   "end": 66.85,
   "text": "But only ten dollars less than a car with a hundred fewer miles."
  },
  {
   "i": 20,
   "start": 67.31,
   "end": 69.46,
   "text": "One digit mattered more than the wear."
  },
  {
   "i": 21,
   "start": 70.14,
   "end": 71.82,
   "text": "Retail tells the same story."
  },
  {
   "i": 22,
   "start": 72.32,
   "end": 77.67,
   "text": "In field experiments, nine endings raised demand every time, especially for new items."
  },
  {
   "i": 23,
   "start": 78.07,
   "end": 84.66,
   "text": "And across seventeen hundred products, shoppers reacted as if that one cent were worth fifteen to twenty-five."
  },
  {
   "i": 24,
   "start": 85.12,
   "end": 87.65,
   "text": "Stores priced as if it were worth two or three."
  },
  {
   "i": 25,
   "start": 88.33,
   "end": 91.48,
   "text": "You may have heard it started so cashiers had to open the register."
  },
  {
   "i": 26,
   "start": 92.0,
   "end": 93.15,
   "text": "It's a popular story."
  },
  {
   "i": 27,
   "start": 93.49,
   "end": 94.94,
   "text": "It's also just a legend."
  },
  {
   "i": 28,
   "start": 95.45,
   "end": 98.22,
   "text": "So next time you see nine ninety-nine, remember:"
  },
  {
   "i": 29,
   "start": 98.72,
   "end": 100.41,
   "text": "the cent isn't saving you much."
  },
  {
   "i": 30,
   "start": 100.7,
   "end": 103.28,
   "text": "It's changing the digit your brain reads first."
  },
  {
   "i": 31,
   "start": 103.71,
   "end": 105.22,
   "text": "The price tag hasn't changed."
  },
  {
   "i": 32,
   "start": 105.6,
   "end": 107.31,
   "text": "The way you read it has."
  }
 ],
